BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: State D'Alembert's Principle. Explain how it converts dynamic kinetics problems into equivalent static equilibrium systems, and demonstrate its formulation with a free-body diagram.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Principle Statement:) Tj
T*
(   D'Alembert's Principle asserts that a system of particles in accelerated motion may be brought) Tj
T*
(   into a state of dynamic equilibrium) Tj
T*
(   by adding to the externally applied active forces and support reactions a fictitious force) Tj
T*
(   termed the 'Reversed Effective Force' or 'Inertia Force':) Tj
T*
(   F_inertia = - m * a.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Mathematical Formulation:) Tj
T*
(   Newton's Second Law: Sigma F = m * a.) Tj
T*
(   Transposing terms: Sigma F - m * a = 0, or Sigma F + F_inertia = 0.) Tj
T*
(   In orthogonal Cartesian components for planar systems:) Tj
T*
(   Sigma F_x + \(- m * a_x\) = 0.) Tj
T*
(   Sigma F_y + \(- m * a_y\) = 0.) Tj
T*
(   Sigma M_O + \(- m * a\) * d = 0 \(where d is the perpendicular moment arm to the inertia force) Tj
T*
(   line of action\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Engineering Significance in Civil & Structural Dynamics:) Tj
T*
(   - Permits structural engineers to treat inertial earthquake loads \(seismic base shear V_b = m *) Tj
T*
(   a_ground\) as equivalent static lateral loads.) Tj
T*
(   - Allows application of established static methods \(Lami's theorem, method of sections,) Tj
T*
(   Varignon's theorem\) directly to dynamic problems.) Tj
T*
(   - Provides intuitive visualization of passenger load shifts in accelerating vehicles and) Tj
T*
(   cranes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 8:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Two blocks A \(mass m_A = 40 kg\) and B \(mass m_B = 25 kg\) are connected by a light inextensible cable passing over a frictionless, massless pulley. Block A rests on a horizontal concrete slab with kinetic friction mu_k = 0.25, while Block B hangs freely. Using D'Alembert's Principle, calculate: \(1\) System linear acceleration a, \(2\) Cable tension T \(take g = 9.81 m/s^2\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Kinematic Constraint:) Tj
T*
(   Since the cable is inextensible, both blocks have identical acceleration magnitude 'a'.) Tj
T*
(   Block A accelerates horizontally to the right \(+ x direction\); Block B accelerates vertically) Tj
T*
(   downward \(+ y direction\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Dynamic Equilibrium of Block A \(Horizontal Slab\):) Tj
T*
(   Forces acting on A:) Tj
T*
(   - Vertical gravity weight: W_A = m_A * g = 40 * 9.81 = 392.4 N \(downward\).) Tj
T*
(   - Normal reaction: N_A = W_A = 392.4 N \(upward\).) Tj
T*
ET

