(   [Problem Statement]: A pile driving hammer of mass m1 = 1200 kg falls freely from height h = 3.0 m and strikes a steel pile of mass m2 = 600 kg. Assuming plastic impact \(coefficient of restitution e = 0\), calculate: \(1\) Velocity of hammer just before impact, \(2\) Common velocity of hammer and pile immediately after impact, \(3\) Loss of mechanical kinetic energy during the collision.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Velocity of Hammer Prior to Impact \(v1\):) Tj
T*
(   v1 = sqrt\( 2 * g * h \) = sqrt\( 2 * 9.81 * 3.0 \) = sqrt\( 58.86 \) = 7.672 m/s.) Tj
T*
(   Initial pile velocity v2 = 0.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Common Post-Impact Velocity \(V_common\) via Momentum Conservation:) Tj
T*
(   During instantaneous impact \(duration dt -> 0\), impulsive forces dwarf external gravity) Tj
T*
(   weights.) Tj
T*
(   m1 * v1 + m2 * v2 = \(m1 + m2\) * V_common.) Tj
T*
(   1200 * 7.672 + 600 * 0 = \(1200 + 600\) * V_common.) Tj
T*
(   9206.4 = 1800 * V_common => V_common = 9206.4 / 1800 = 5.115 m/s =~ 5.12 m/s.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

