(   [Problem Statement]: A civil construction safety net is designed to arrest a falling worker of mass m = 80 kg who falls from a bridge scaffold height h = 6.0 m. The safety net behaves as a non-linear spring absorbing kinetic energy with vertical deflection Delta y = 1.5 m before bringing the worker to rest. Calculate: \(1\) Total work done by gravity, \(2\) Average arresting force exerted by the safety net, \(3\) Peak deceleration experienced in terms of 'g'.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Work Done by Gravity:) Tj
T*
(   Total vertical drop = free fall height h + net deflection Delta y = 6.0 + 1.5 = 7.5 m.) Tj
T*
(   W_gravity = m * g * \(h + Delta y\) = 80 * 9.81 * 7.5 = 784.8 * 7.5 = 5886.0 Joules \(5.886 kJ\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Average Arresting Force \(F_avg\):) Tj
T*
(   By the Work-Energy Theorem \(initial and final velocities are zero: u = 0, v = 0\):) Tj
T*
(   Sigma U_1-2 = Delta KE = 0.) Tj
T*
(   W_gravity - F_avg * Delta y = 0 => F_avg = W_gravity / Delta y.) Tj
T*
(   F_avg = 5886.0 / 1.5 = 3924.0 N = 3.924 kN.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Net Average Upward Deceleration:) Tj
T*
(   Net upward force during arrest: F_net = F_avg - m*g = 3924.0 - 784.8 = 3139.2 N.) Tj
T*
(   a_avg = F_net / m = 3139.2 / 80 = 39.24 m/s^2.) Tj
T*
(   In terms of g: a_avg / g = 39.24 / 9.81 = 4.0 g.) Tj
T*
(   Conclusion: Work of gravity = 5.89 kJ; Average arresting force = 3.92 kN; Average deceleration) Tj
T*
(   = 4.0 g.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
